---
const notes =
    "I'm Rubbie, a software engineer from Nigeria. (talk about the topic). My stack is Rust and Postgres, so that's what the demos run on, The ideas are the same in any language with async/await features, and in any database. I've simplified the scenarios on purpose so you can follow along even if you don't write code. (3) The title sounds dramatic. It isn't. It's two things, each waiting on the other, so neither ever moves. And that's what makes it so tricky: it's simple enough to describe, and in a big system it's still easy to build without noticing. \"By the end you won't just have a story, you'll have a method for taking one of these apart";
---

<div class="s title-slide">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-[1em]">
        <img src="/rubbie-icon.png" alt="" class="title-avatar" />
        <div>
            <p class="strong">
                rubbie kelvin<span class="s-accent">.</span>
            </p>
            <p class="s-muted">software engineer, nigeria.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-auto">
        <p class="s-kicker">sysconf · 2026</p>
        <h1 class="title-main">
            the autopsy of an asynchronous deadlock
            <span class="s-accent">.</span>
        </h1>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-auto flex justify-between s-small">
        <span>rubbietheone.com</span>
        <span>examples in rust · ideas for every language</span>
    </div>
    {import.meta.env.DEV && (
        <aside class="s-notes" hidden>
            {notes}
        </aside>
    )}
</div>

<style>
    .title-slide {
        padding: 4em 5em;
    }
    .title-avatar {
        width: 3em;
        height: 3em;
        border-radius: 9999px;
    }
    .title-main {
        font-size: 3em;
        font-weight: 500;
        line-height: 1.1;
        max-width: 18em;
    }
</style>
